WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.429
Prompt structure for RAG.

2
00:00:01.709 --> 00:00:04.717
The layout of a RAG prompt is not a matter of taste.

3
00:00:04.997 --> 00:00:09.307
Anthropic’s long-context guidance makes two concrete recommendations.

4
00:00:09.587 --> 00:00:22.771
Put long documents near the top, above the question and instructions: in Anthropic’s tests, queries at the end improved response quality by up to 30 percent, especially with complex multi-document inputs.

5
00:00:23.051 --> 00:00:30.624
And wrap each document in its own tags with its metadata (a source, an index) so the model can tell passages apart.

6
00:00:30.904 --> 00:00:43.555
This connects to the “lost in the middle” result from Studio 1: models use information unevenly across long inputs, so make the structure easy to navigate and send only what the question needs.

7
00:00:43.835 --> 00:00:52.517
Give every passage a stable id that your code controls, such as travel-002 or pep-0008#s3.

8
00:00:52.797 --> 00:00:56.424
The id is what the model cites and what your verifier checks.

9
00:00:56.704 --> 00:01:03.765
A position number (“document 4”) is fragile, because the same chunk sits at a different position on every query.

10
00:01:04.045 --> 00:01:05.240
Then write the rules.

11
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.845
Answer only from the provided sources.

12
00:01:08.125 --> 00:01:09.853
Cite the source of each statement.

13
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:14.507
If sources conflict, prefer the newer one and say an older one disagrees.

14
00:01:14.787 --> 00:01:20.589
If the sources don’t contain the answer, reply with one exact sentence you can detect in code.

15
00:01:20.869 --> 00:01:32.027
Anthropic’s guidance on reducing hallucinations recommends exactly these moves: give the model explicit permission to say it doesn’t know, and restrict it to the provided documents.

16
00:01:32.307 --> 00:01:36.061
One more rule matters because your corpus is untrusted input.

17
00:01:36.341 --> 00:01:43.125
Retrieved text can contain instructions (“ignore previous instructions and…”), whether planted or accidental.

18
00:01:43.405 --> 00:01:52.771
Keep your rules in the system prompt, keep passages clearly delimited as data, and never let the content of a passage change what tools the model can call.

19
00:01:53.051 --> 00:01:57.040
Studio 1’s prompt injection material applies here unchanged.
